This program demonstrates the implementation and performance comparison of **Max Heap Sort and Min Heap Sort** using C++. The program first asks the user to enter the number of elements and then generates a set of random integer values using the `rand()` function. The generated array is stored as the original array, and two copies are created so that both sorting techniques can be applied to the same input data. This makes the comparison between the two methods fair and consistent.

In **Max Heap Sort**, the program creates a **Max Heap**, where the parent node is greater than its child nodes. The `maxHeapify()` function maintains the max-heap property by comparing a node with its left and right children and moving the largest value to the correct position. After building the max heap, the largest element at the root is repeatedly swapped with the last element of the unsorted portion of the array. The heap size is then reduced and `maxHeapify()` is called again. This process continues until the complete array is sorted.

In **Min Heap Sort**, the program follows a similar process but creates a **Min Heap**, where the parent node is smaller than its child nodes. The `minHeapify()` function compares the current element with its children and places the smallest element at the correct position. The minimum element is repeatedly moved to the end of the array. Since this produces the elements in reverse order for the required output, the `reverse()` function is used at the end to obtain the array in ascending order.

The program uses the **C++ `chrono` library** to measure the execution time of both sorting algorithms. The time is calculated using `high_resolution_clock` and displayed in **nanoseconds and microseconds**. Separate start and end times are recorded for Max Heap Sort and Min Heap Sort, allowing their execution performance to be compared. 

Both Max Heap Sort and Min Heap Sort use heapification and have a time complexity of **O(n log n)**. The heap construction and repeated removal of the root element make heap sort suitable for efficiently sorting large amounts of data. The program therefore helps demonstrate the working of heap-based sorting, the difference between Max Heap and Min Heap approaches, and the practical execution time of both methods.

**Overall, this program provides a practical comparison of Max Heap Sort and Min Heap Sort by using the same randomly generated input array and measuring their execution time. It shows how heaps can be used to efficiently organize and sort data while maintaining an O(n log n) time complexity.**
